  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nWith sunset and loss of daytime heating, convective coverage \nhas waned some overall. That said, an additional vort max \nembedded within broad troughing is expected to reinvigorate \nshowers and thunderstorms after midnight tonight. Any severe \nthreat will be low with the nocturnally stable profile, though \nthe column remains quite juicy, and longer deep layer moisture \ntransport vectors support a slight uptick in PWAT values inching\nback up to 1.8-1.9\" within southwesterly flow, so these will \nlikely be efficient rainfall producers and pose some additional \nflood threat especially if they move over areas that were hit \nhard on Friday.\n\nThese showers/storms are likely to continue into early Saturday\nas the shortwave completes its passage. After a lull in the \nactivity for part of the day, a stronger shortwave within the \ntrough is progged to cross the Upper Ohio Valley region late \nSaturday into Saturday night, with additional showers and \nthunderstorms, along with the continued potential for gusty wind\nand heavy downpours. \n\nGuidance continues to trend toward a dry forecast on Sunday,\nwith brief shortwave ridging behind the stronger Saturday\nshortwave. Dry air and some capping should preclude convection\nthrough the day. \n\nThe broad trough is reestablished across the Great Lakes and\nUpper Ohio Valley region early next week, with daily shower and\nthunderstorm chances returning. Currently, machine learning \nindicates a greater potential for severe weather during this \nperiod, though significant uncertainty remains in the details.",
